 "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fWB9blq0H34",
 "title": "The Trap of the Word But",
 "topic": "mindset",
 "summary": "The creator discusses the philosophical experiment of Buridan's donkey to illustrate how people get trapped by wanting two conflicting things. The main lesson is that using the word \"but\" to balance contradictory desires leads to inaction and failure.",
 "key_points": [
  "In Buridan's philosophy experiment, a donkey is placed halfway between carrots and water.",
  "The donkey gets trapped because moving toward the water means moving away from the carrots, and vice versa, leading to hunger and thirst.",
  "People do the same thing using the single word \"but\" to hold conflicting desires.",
  "Examples of conflicting desires include wanting a creative life \"but\" never wanting to get rejected, or wanting to run a marathon \"but\" not wanting to get tired.",
  "People often hide the \"but\" and do not say it out loud."
 ],
 "content": "The creator introduces an actual carrot and an actual water bottle, and a golden object representing a donkey for a thought experiment. He describes \"Buridan's philosophy experiment from a couple hundred years ago\", where a donkey is hungry and thirsty and has complete freedom to move. The donkey is halfway between a pile of carrots and a bucket of water. The creator claims the donkey is trapped because moving toward the water moves him away from the carrot, which he cannot do because he is hungry, and vice versa. As a result of being trapped, the experiment ends with the donkey dying of hunger and thirst. The creator claims that all people do this with one simple word, which is \"but\". People say, \"I want this, but I also want that, right?\" He provides examples: \"I want to have a creative life, but I never want to get rejected,\" and \"I want to run the marathon, but I don't want to get tired.\" The creator states that people hide the \"but\" and do not say it out loud.",
